paper/sections/3_method.tex:165:\paragraph{Ensembling with Direct inside v10.}
paper/sections/3_method.tex:166:The v9 agent alone does not uniformly beat Direct across backbones (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: $+3.64$/$+3.68$/$-4.46$ pp on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5). v10 therefore always averages the v9 output with an \emph{independent} parallel Direct call from the same VLM backbone:
paper/sections/3_method.tex:168:  s_{\mathrm{final}} \;=\; 0.5 \cdot s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5 \cdot s_{v9},
paper/sections/3_method.tex:170:with $\alpha{=}0.5$ fixed a priori. This always-on design (described in \S\ref{ssec:v10}) is what produces the significant ensemble gains in Table~\ref{tab:main_results} on all three backbones, including Qwen3.5 where the agent alone is individually worse than Direct.
paper/sections/3_method.tex:173:Section~\ref{ssec:score_diversity} isolates the causal driver of the ensemble gain via rank-preserving and rank-collapsing transformations of the agent's scores. In an earlier draft on Qwen3.5 we measured Direct at $11$ unique scores on the test set and a free-form JSON agent at $49$; averaging the two fills in the rank ties inside Direct's coarse bimodal clusters with real signal. Transformations that preserve the agent's unique-value grid retain the full gain; a median-split binarisation that collapses the grid to $2$ values cuts the gain in half. Section~\ref{ssec:score_diversity} retains that analysis as legacy evidence for the rank-granularity mechanism; the mechanism is agent-family agnostic and applies to v9 as well, although the absolute unique-value count differs by runner.
paper/sections/3_method.tex:180:    \item \textbf{Bounded call budget.} v10 issues 1 Direct call plus $1$--$5$ v9 agent turns per item; the average across CrossDomainVAD-12 is $\approx 1.0 + 1.8 = 2.8$ VLM calls/item with the Direct and v9 branches running concurrently ($\sim 1.8$ wall-time calls due to parallel execution).
