Score: 7.6/10
Verdict: almost
Verified claims: R6 W5 is substantively fixed. `v11_frozen_no_rules`, `v11_frozen_meta_only`, and `v11_frozen_shuffled` contain the same matched item IDs and identical cached `v9_score`, `direct_score`, branch rationales, and labels when joined by `item_id`. Row order differs, but `v11_bootstrap_5way.py` joins by `item_id`, so the paired comparison is valid. I reproduced the 5-way bootstrap numbers: full v11 `0.7539`, blend `0.7333`, no-rules `0.7227`, shuffled `0.7199`, meta-only `0.7388`; full vs blend `+2.05 pp`, CI `[+0.41,+3.67]`, `P=0.995`. The D4 shuffled spot-check also matches: blend `0.6703`, shuffled `0.5776`, delta `−9.26 pp`. The D4 shuffled rulebook is legitimately sourced from D10: top-level `domain_code` rewritten to `D4`, `_shuffled_origin=D10`, and rule contents equal original D10.

Unverified / false claims: The conclusion still falsely says v10 “significantly beats Direct on every backbone” even though Qwen3.5 CI is `[-0.08,+3.51]`. `paper/sections/3_method.tex` also still says Table 1 gives significant ensemble gains on all three backbones. The title and framing still say “Training-Free” while §4’s v11 is explicitly semi-supervised with 480 dev labels. The abstract and intro still present v10 as the only main finding and do not integrate the now-central v11 controller result, so §4 is technically strong but narratively orphaned.

Weaknesses (ranked, with minimum fix each)

1. Blocking factual/narrative inconsistency around “training-free” and significance. Minimum fix: change title or subtitle to separate “training-free v10 base” from “semi-supervised v11 controller extension,” and replace every “significant on every backbone” claim with “significant on two of three; Qwen positive-in-mean but CI touches zero.”

2. Abstract/intro/conclusion still do not integrate v11. Minimum fix: add one concise abstract sentence and one intro contribution bullet for v11: on Qwen3.5, a semi-supervised branch-frozen controller adds `+2.05 pp` over v10, and shuffled/no-rule controls show rule content, not controller mechanics, carries the gain.

3. Shuffled control is legitimate but not perfectly length/content matched. The symmetric D1↔D7...D6↔D12 mapping is valid, but category filtering means some shuffled domains receive mostly wrong routing meta-rules rather than rich wrong domain content. Minimum fix: state this explicitly and frame shuffled as a wrong-domain RAG control, not a strict token-length-matched placebo. If space allows, report average retrieved token count per regime.

4. D7/D8 positive shuffled behavior does not kill the story, but it weakens the blanket wording “Controller is reading rule content.” On D7/D8, the controller can still win by recognizing branch imbalance even under irrelevant rules. Minimum fix: phrase the claim as “domain-matched rule content is necessary for the full macro gain and for content-dependent domains,” not “every gain comes from content reading.”

5. v11 evidence is still only Qwen3.5. This is acceptable as an extension but not as a new universal headline. Minimum fix: say v11 is a Qwen3.5 controller-learning case study unless the authors run at least one more backbone.

Memory update for Round 8 (if any)
R7 technical ablation is accepted: branch-frozen pairing and shuffled negative control are reproducible and materially fix R6 W5/W6. Remaining blockers are presentation, not core evidence: fix title/abstract/intro/conclusion training-free framing, remove false “significant on all three” wording, and qualify shuffled-control interpretation because D7/D8 improve despite wrong rules.
